$string['promptapireference'] = 'Before creating HTML or JavaScript, read the complete mod_mcp API catalogue at {$a} and use only the operations documented there. For interactive lessons with steps, load MoodleMCP.progress.getStatus() when opening and save with MoodleMCP.progress.saveStatus({version, location, percent, currentstep, totalsteps}) after meaningful changes. Use the version returned by the most recent read/save, and reload on a conflict. Show the learner their current position and allow resuming. Never treat a saved 100% as native Moodle completion or grading; inspect completion.get() and follow its rules. Do not store the learner\'s only progress in localStorage.';
$string['promptblum'] = 'PEDAGOGICAL GUIDANCE - LEARNING LEVEL. While gathering the initial requirements for an educational activity, ask the teacher: "What level of learning would you like to work on?" Briefly explain: "Choose what you expect students to be able to do after the activity. This guides the content, challenges and interactions." Offer these selectable options with their complete descriptions, using chat selection controls if available or a numbered list otherwise: Automatic (recommended): Let AI choose the most suitable level based on the subject and the learning objective. Progressive learning: Start with basic concepts and gradually move towards more challenging activities. 1. Remember: Help students recognize, identify and recall important information about the topic. 2. Understand: Help students grasp concepts and explain them in their own words. 3. Apply: Ask students to use what they learned to solve practical situations. 4. Analyze: Encourage students to investigate problems, compare information and identify causes and relationships. 5. Evaluate: Present situations in which students must make decisions and justify their choices. 6. Create: Challenge students to develop ideas, design projects or build their own solutions. Allow a free-text choice as well. If the teacher does not specify a level, use Automatic without insisting. In I choose mode, FIRST ask what kind of activity the teacher wants, then ask about the learning level when relevant. For satisfaction surveys intended only to collect opinions, DO NOT ask for a learning level or apply cognitive classification. Use the revised Bloom taxonomy internally to align observable objectives, activities, feedback and assessment with the selected level, without showing the technical taxonomy name to the teacher or learners. Do not confuse difficulty with learning level, and do not force all six levels. In Progressive mode, choose a sequence appropriate for the learners and objective. Wait for the other teacher responses and reference materials before generating content.';
$string['promptcase'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Build a DECISION-BASED CASE STUDY WITH CONSEQUENCES. Ask the teacher for the scenario, audience, learning objective, factual context, approximate number of decision points and whether choices can be revisited. Present the initial situation and ONE decision per step with plausible alternatives, distinct branches, coherent consequences and explanatory feedback. Include both successful and unsuccessful outcomes without dead ends; allow revisiting the decision path and retrying other choices when appropriate. Model a finite, consistent decision graph; verify all possible paths and avoid references to nonexistent steps. Save the individual step, selections and decision path using MoodleMCP.progress.load()/save() with version checks and conflict handling; update percentage via progress.saveStatus() sequentially when useful. Do not call the simulation an official graded assessment: if grading is requested, use questions from activity JSON with server-side grading, never a score computed inside the iframe. Produce complete, accessible, responsive HTML. {$a->blum}';
$string['promptclicktocopy'] = 'Click or tap the text to select all and copy automatically.';
$string['promptcolours'] = 'MANDATORY BRAND COLOUR RULE for the {$a->theme} theme: the variables {$a->variables} are the only sources of brand colours. Do not invent an alternative palette or hardcode hex, rgb()/rgba(), hsl()/hsla() or named brand colours for hero sections, gradients and every gradient stop, buttons, links, borders, icons, cards, hover/focus states or pseudo-elements. All brand colours MUST use var({$a->primary}, #0d6efd) and var({$a->complementary}, var({$a->primary}, #0d6efd)), or local tokens derived from these variables. Valid example: .hero { background: linear-gradient(123deg, var({$a->primary}, #0d6efd), var({$a->complementary}, var({$a->primary}, #0d6efd))); }. For intermediate tones and transparency, use color-mix() with these variables. Use the matching -rgb channel variables listed above for translucent brand colours, for example rgba(var(--bs-primary-rgb), .12); do not hardcode RGB channel values or invent a different palette. Literal colours are allowed only as fallbacks inside var() or for neutrals and semantic accessibility states, never as substitutes for theme brand colours. Before delivering the HTML, audit the entire CSS, including media queries and pseudo-elements, and replace any invented brand colours. Mod MCP passes the current theme variable values to the isolated iframe in the Moodle browser; fallbacks remain necessary for standalone previews and the app.';
$string['promptcustom'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). This is the I CHOOSE mode: the FIRST ChatGPT or Claude response must ask the teacher directly "What kind of activity would you like to create?" and invite unrestricted free-text directions describing the experience, its content and its learning objective. Examples may inspire the teacher, but never limit the answer to predefined choices or assume a quiz, page or other format. Ask whether there are documents or reference materials, and WAIT for the responses before planning, generating content or calling write tools. Then clarify only the requirements relevant to the chosen format and create a complete HTML experience, with CSS and JavaScript when useful, following the documented mod_mcp APIs. If a graded assessment is requested, use JSON questions with server-side grading, never client-side grading. Preserve teacher freedom while respecting security, privacy, accessibility, progress and the activity-wide instructions. {$a->blum}';
$string['promptdarkmode'] = 'CSS MUST support both light and dark mode: define light styles by default, use @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) for system preferences, and support explicit [data-bs-theme="dark"] and [data-bs-theme="light"] overrides where available. The HTML runs in an isolated iframe, so include its own theme styles rather than relying on attributes or CSS inherited from the parent Moodle page. Ensure readable backgrounds, text, borders and controls with sufficient contrast.';
$string['promptdescriptioncase'] = 'Create a case study where learners make decisions, explore consequences and receive feedback on their choices. ChatGPT or Claude will ask: "What scenario and factual context?", "Who is the audience and what is the learning objective?", "How many decisions should be included?", "Can learners go back and reconsider choices?" and "Do you have documents or examples to refer to?". The decision paths will have consistent branches and feedback.';
$string['promptdescriptioncustom'] = 'Build the learning activity you have in mind, without being limited to the other tabs. ChatGPT or Claude will first ask: "What kind of activity do you want to create?". Based on your answer, it will ask: "How should it work?", "What are the content, audience and learning objective?", "What interactions should it include?" and "Do you have documents, images or other references?". It will wait for your answers before generating content.';
$string['promptdescriptioninteractive'] = 'Create an interactive quiz with questions in a fixed or randomized order, graded by Moodle. Before starting, ChatGPT or Claude will ask: "What topic, audience and learning objective?", "How many questions do you want?", "Should questions be shuffled?", "How many should be randomly selected per attempt?" and "Do you have files or reference materials?". Answer keys stay in server-side JSON, not in the HTML.';
$string['promptdescriptionpage'] = 'Build an educational content page with explanations, examples and visuals, without grades or assessment questions. ChatGPT or Claude will ask: "Would you prefer an explanatory lesson, step-by-step tutorial, summary, case study or another format?", "What topic, audience and learning objective?", "Any specific instructions?" and "Do you have PDFs, images or other reference materials?". The page will follow your answers.';
$string['promptdescriptionsimulation'] = 'Create an interactive simulation in which learners change parameters and observe results, graphs or diagrams. ChatGPT or Claude will ask: "What phenomenon or process should be simulated?", "Who is the audience?", "Which values can learners change?", "What units, formulas and safe limits should apply?" and "Which outputs should be shown?". If reliable rules are missing, it will request sources and assumptions before building the model.';
$string['promptdescriptionsurvey'] = 'Build a satisfaction survey that presents one question at a time and saves responses without grades. ChatGPT or Claude will ask: "What is the purpose of the survey?", "Who will respond?", "How many questions?", "What scales or response formats should be used?", "Which answers are required?" and "Do you need reports or anonymity?". Consolidated reports and anonymity are not guaranteed without additional server-side support.';
$string['promptdescriptiontimeline'] = 'Create an explorable timeline of events organized by date, with details learners can inspect. ChatGPT or Claude will ask: "What topic and time period?", "Which key events should appear?", "Who is the audience?", "Would you prefer event cards, expandable details or filters?" and "Are there sources or reference materials?". Dates and other facts must be verifiable.';
$string['promptfreedom'] = '<strong>These are only suggestions:</strong> you do not have to limit yourself to them. Ask the AI to add other features, interactions, or any ideas you have. The only limit is your imagination.';
$string['promptimagealt'] = 'Visual example of {$a}.';
$string['promptinstructions'] = 'Before generating any content, questions, HTML, CSS or JavaScript, ASK THE TEACHER what they actually want to create in this activity, which topics to cover and what the learning objective is. Never infer the content from the activity title alone. Also ask whether they have documents, PDFs, images or other reference materials to use as a starting point; if they do, ask them to upload or share these files and review their contents before proceeding, without inventing information from unavailable files. Wait for the teacher\'s response and any materials they choose to provide before planning or creating anything. After that, ask any further questions needed to configure the activity, if applicable, and only then begin creating it. This activity will be EMBEDDED inside an EXISTING Moodle page that already displays the course name, activity title, navigation and theme interface. Use the Moodle address, course and course-module IDs, activity name and URL ONLY as technical context to locate and edit the activity through MCP tools. DO NOT reproduce these details in student-visible HTML and do not add a top metadata banner, institutional header, cover, breadcrumb, menu or navigation bar that duplicates the Moodle interface. DO NOT create ANY FOOTER in the HTML, even one that differs from the Moodle footer: do not use the <footer> tag or sections, divs or visual bars serving as a footer. Do not add credits, signatures, copyright notices, institutional closing messages, author names, support links or bottom navigation. If reference materials contain a footer, do not reproduce it. Before saving content, review and remove any footer; end the page immediately after the final educational section or last necessary interactive control. Do not create cards, labels, badges or text such as "Moodle", "Course", "Course ID", "Module ID" or the activity URL as metadata. Do not repeat the activity title at the top of the HTML just because it appears in the prompt. Start the embedded page directly with the requested educational content, using internal section headings only where pedagogically useful. Keep the HTML document complete, with its own styles for the isolated iframe, without duplicating the surrounding platform layout. The interactive document has an asynchronous window.MoodleMCP SDK exposed inside its sandbox. Use MoodleMCP.course.getActivities() and getNavigation() for allowed course links; MoodleMCP.completion.get() and complete() to respect the configured native completion rules; MoodleMCP.progress.load() and save({version, location, data, userdata}) to resume user progress; MoodleMCP.attempt.get()/start()/finish() and MoodleMCP.questions.get()/submit(responses, finish) for supported activity JSON question interactions; MoodleMCP.tracking.record({type, action, object, result}) for bounded learning events. These operations are asynchronous and can be denied by Moodle permissions. Never place session tokens, correct answers or server-side grading logic in the HTML. Do not assume completion is enabled or that quiz grading is performed by authored HTML. The JSON assessment is graded by the server. Present the complete proposed content and settings in one preview before writing. Offer Approve and Reject in the conversation, not in the HTML. Save directly only after explicit approval; if rejected, leave Moodle unchanged. Instructions in this prompt are intended solely to guide page generation. DO NOT turn them into text, notices, labels, captions or other visible elements in the HTML.';
$string['promptpage'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit the EXISTING activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). In your FIRST response, before writing HTML, planning content or calling write tools, ask the teacher to choose a page format: explanatory lesson, step-by-step tutorial, summary, case study, or other. Offer selectable choices AND a free-text input field where the teacher can describe the topic, audience, learning objective and specific directions. Also ask whether they have documents, PDFs, images or other reference materials. If the chat interface supports choices and text input, present both in the same interaction; otherwise offer numbered options and explicitly invite an open-ended text response. Never restrict the teacher to predefined choices. Wait for the teacher\'s answers and any shared materials before creating content . Build an educational CONTENT PAGE, not a quiz or assessment. Create a complete, accessible, responsive HTML document organized into meaningful sections with headings, explanations, practical examples and helpful visual elements as appropriate, including CSS in <style> and JavaScript in <script> when useful. Do not create graded questions, scoring, answer keys or question bank attachments. Present the complete content preview and obtain approval before saving directly with mcp_save_content. If rejected, leave Moodle unchanged. {$a->blum}';
$string['promptquiz'] = 'Using the connection to {$a->wwwroot}, course {$a->courseid}, edit the existing mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (cmid {$a->cmid}, {$a->url}). Ask the teacher how many questions, whether to shuffle and whether to draw a random subset; wait for answers. Generate questions directly in private JSON information using type quiz and information containing selection all or random, count, shuffle, questions. Every question has id, text, type (single, multiple or text), answer, points, and options with id and text when relevant. Do NOT use the Moodle Question Bank, mcp_create_question or mcp_attach_question. Keep answer keys solely in server JSON, never authored HTML. Use MoodleMCP.attempt and MoodleMCP.questions for server-controlled selection and grading; never grade inside the iframe. Create accessible responsive HTML. Present the complete content and assessment configuration for approval before saving together with mcp_save_content, including type, information and expectedhash. {$a->blum}';
