$string['promptsimulation'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Build an INTERACTIVE SIMULATION in which learners change parameters and observe outcomes. Ask the teacher for the phenomenon or process, audience, input parameters, units, rules or formulas, safe bounds and expected outputs. Include accessible inputs (fields, selectors or sliders), current values, outputs and helpful graphs or diagrams, presets, a reset control and explanations of each change. Validate inputs and bounds, keep units consistent, handle invalid states and avoid ungrounded models; ask the teacher for trustworthy assumptions if reliable formulas are missing before implementing. Clearly label simplifications and assumptions and never present the simulation as real-world measurement. Save scenarios and parameters with MoodleMCP.progress.load()/save() and version conflict handling; for guided sequences, use progress.getStatus()/saveStatus() with fresh versions. Never assign official grades based on HTML calculations. Generate complete, accessible, responsive HTML, CSS and JavaScript. {$a->blum}';
$string['promptsurvey'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Build a SATISFACTION SURVEY, not a graded quiz. Ask the teacher about its purpose, audience, number of questions, scales/response types, required answers and any need for reports or anonymity. Display exactly ONE question at a time. For single-choice answers, immediately prevent duplicate selections, persist the answer and ONLY after a successful save animate the selected question out (for example opacity and translation) and the next one in; do not require a Next button for single-choice answers. For free text or multi-select use a clear confirmation button before advancing. Respect prefers-reduced-motion, keyboard navigation, focus, screen readers, validation and save-error states; never advance or lose an answer when persistence fails. Show progress, allow resuming after reloading, and display a final confirmation. Save type survey and its questions using mcp_save_information. Use MoodleMCP.progress.load()/save({version, location, data, userdata}) to persist per-learner answers in userdata and their position; if also updating structured progress using progress.saveStatus(), use the version returned by the preceding save and never issue concurrent writes. Respect API storage limits and do not place unnecessary personal data into tracking events. The current API has no consolidated teacher response reporting and does not guarantee anonymity: if these are required, explain that backend support is necessary instead of promising them using HTML/JavaScript alone. Do not create graded questions, answer keys or official grades. {$a->blum}';
$string['prompttimeline'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Build an explorable educational TIMELINE. Ask the teacher for the subject, timeframe, key events, audience and display preferences. Arrange events in verified chronological order with date/period, title, description and sources when provided; never fabricate dates or facts. Provide responsive desktop and mobile views with expandable events, accessible keyboard navigation, sufficient contrast and readable text. Add period or category filters when useful. Allow revisiting events without losing state, and save the learner position with MoodleMCP.progress.getStatus()/saveStatus() when the timeline has sequential steps, respecting versions and conflicts. Reading progress is not native Moodle completion. Generate a complete HTML document with appropriate CSS and JavaScript, without a quiz or grades by default. {$a->blum}';
